{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b96002e216e227a1", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tDialectS2S: End-to-End Speech Dialogue\n2\tModeling for Low-Resource Chinese Dialects\n3\tYi Shu1 , Tianyu Peng1,2,3 , Yingzhuo Deng1,2 , Wen Yang1,2 , Jun Lin4 ,\n4\tChangming Xie4 , Xinyu Yu4 , and Jiajun Zhang1,2,3(B)\n5\t\n6\tarXiv:2608.08067v1 [cs.CL] 8 Aug 2026\n7\t\n8\t1\n9\t\n10\tSchool of Artificial Intelligence, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences\n11\t2\n12\tInstitute of Automation, Chinese Academy of Sciences\n13\t3\n14\tWuhan AI Research\n15\t4\n16\tGWM AI Lab\n17\tshuyi23@mails.ucas.ac.cn jjzhang@nlpr.ia.ac.cn\n18\t\n19\tAbstract. Current end-to-end speech dialogue models are primarily optimized for mainstream languages and remain limited in low-resource\n20\tdialect scenarios due to the scarcity of dialect speech data. Moreover,\n21\tduring dialect adaptation, the semantic representation space of speech\n22\tdialogue models continuously evolves, while conventional speech supervision remains unchanged, leading to semantic inconsistency between\n23\thidden representations and speech targets and degrading speech stability and naturalness. To address these issues, we propose DialectS2S, an\n24\tend-to-end speech dialogue model for Chinese dialects. We first develop\n25\ta scalable dialect speech dialogue synthesis pipeline for efficient data\n26\tconstruction. We further introduce a two-stage post-training strategy\n27\twith self-aligned speech supervision, which aligns the semantic content\n28\tof speech supervision with the evolved semantic representations of the\n29\tmodel to improve dialect speech generation quality. Experimental results\n30\tshow that DialectS2S consistently outperforms existing baselines across\n31\tmultiple Chinese dialects in speech dialogue, achieving substantial improvements in dialect consistency, response quality, and speech intelligibility. Our work provides an efficient and scalable solution for end-to-end\n32\tspeech dialogue modeling in low-resource dialect scenarios. To facilitate\n33\tfuture research and practical applications, we fully open-source the DialectS2S framework, including model checkpoints, training datasets, and\n34\tfine-tuning code.\n35\tKeywords: Speech-to-Speech Dialogue · Chinese Dialects · Self-aligned\n36\tSpeech Supervision · End-to-End Large Speech Language Models\n37\t\n38\t1\n39\t\n40\tIntroduction\n41\t\n42\tDialects play an important role in regional communication and cultural expression. Recent end-to-end speech dialogue models [23,19,6,18,17,3] have achieved\n43\t(B)\n44\t\n45\tCorresponding author\n46\tResources: model checkpoints, training datasets, and fine-tuning code.\n47\t\n48\t\f2\n49\t\n50\tY. Shu et al.\n51\t\n52\tstrong conversational performance in mainstream languages such as Mandarin\n53\tChinese and English. However, support for low-resource Chinese dialects remains\n54\tlimited. Existing Chinese dialect speech research mainly focuses on speech recognition or speech synthesis, while unified end-to-end speech dialogue modeling for\n55\tChinese dialects remains largely underexplored.\n56\tCurrent speech dialogue models often rely on large-scale speech dialogue\n57\tcorpora, while high-quality dialect speech data remain scarce. Moreover, recent\n58\tstudies have shown that directly supervised fine-tuning speech dialogue models\n59\tmay degrade speech quality and generation stability [24]. This issue becomes\n60\tmore challenging in low-resource dialect scenarios due to limited high-quality\n61\tsupervision and large acoustic variations. Therefore, enabling stable and natural\n62\tdialect speech interaction under limited supervision remains a major challenge\n63\tfor end-to-end speech dialogue systems.\n64\tTo address these challenges, we propose DialectS2S, an end-to-end speech\n65\tdialogue model for low-resource Chinese dialects. DialectS2S supports multilingual interaction across five languages and dialects, including Mandarin, English,\n66\tSichuanese, Cantonese, and the Tianjin dialect. To construct dialect speech dialogue data, we develop a scalable synthesis pipeline that rewrites existing dialogue texts into dialectal expressions using large language models and synthesizes\n67\tcorresponding dialect speech with speech generation models. Furthermore, we introduce a two-stage post-training strategy with self-aligned speech supervision to\n68\timprove speech generation stability and naturalness during dialect adaptation.\n69\tIn summary, our main contributions are as follows:\n70\t(1) End-to-End Speech Dialogue Model for Chinese Dialects: We propose DialectS2S, an end-to-end speech dialogue model for low-resource Chinese dialects. To facilitate future research on dialect speech interaction, we\n71\tfully open-source the DialectS2S framework, including model checkpoints,\n72\ttraining datasets, and fine-tuning code.\n73\t(2) Self-Aligned Speech Supervision for Dialect Speech Generation:\n74\tWe propose a two-stage post-training strategy with self-aligned speech supervision, which aligns speech targets with the model’s semantic predictions\n75\tand improves speech intelligibility while preserving response quality.\n76\t(3) Strong Multi-Dialect Performance: Experimental results demonstrate\n77\tthat DialectS2S significantly outperforms existing open-source baselines in\n78\tdialect consistency, response quality, and speech intelligibility across multiple\n79\tChinese dialects.\n80\t\n81\t2\n82\t\n83\tRelated Work\n84\t\n85\t2.1\n86\t\n87\tEnd-to-End Large Speech Models\n88\t\n89\tRecent end-to-end large speech models have achieved strong performance in\n90\tspeech recognition, synthesis, and dialogue tasks. Compared with cascaded systems, these models directly map speech inputs to text and speech outputs, reducing error accumulation and improving interaction naturalness. LLaMA-Omni [8]\n91\t\n92\t\fDialectS2S for Low-Resource Chinese Dialects\n93\t\n94\t3\n95\t\n96\tappends a speech decoder to a large language model for unified speech understanding and generation. GLM-4-Voice [23] adopts interleaved text-speech modeling for streaming interaction. Step-Audio 2 [18] and Step-Audio-R1 [16] follow a\n97\tsimilar interleaved architecture. Moshi [5], Voila [14] and MiniCPM-o [3] further\n98\tsupport low-latency full-duplex conversations. More recently, Qwen2.5-Omni [19]\n99\tand Qwen3-Omni [20] adopt the Thinker-Talker architecture, where a large language model handles semantic reasoning and a smaller model generates speech.\n100\tOpenS2S [17] further improves fine-grained speech understanding and releases\n101\topen-source models and training pipelines. Despite these advances, existing systems are mainly optimized for high-resource languages such as Mandarin and\n102\tEnglish, while low-resource dialect speech interaction remains underexplored.\n103\t2.2\n104\t\n105\tLow-Resource Dialect Speech Modeling\n106\t\n107\tCompared with high-resource languages such as English and Mandarin Chinese,\n108\tspeech modeling for Chinese dialect varieties faces challenges including limited\n109\tdata availability, expensive annotation, and substantial pronunciation variation.\n110\tExisting studies mainly focus on dialect speech corpora, automatic speech recognition (ASR), and text-to-speech synthesis (TTS). KeSpeech [15] constructs a\n111\tlarge-scale corpus covering eight Chinese dialects and more than 27,000 speakers, while WenetSpeech-Yue [9] and WenetSpeech-Chuan [4] provide high-quality\n112\tCantonese and Sichuanese speech datasets. Systems such as FunASR [1] and\n113\tFireRedASR2S [21] support multilingual and multi-dialect speech processing. In\n114\tTTS, DIAMOE-TTS [2] enables unified dialect modeling through IPA representations, while CosyVoice2 [7] improves speech naturalness and speaker consistency\n115\tand supports dialect speech generation from reference audio. However, existing\n116\twork primarily addresses speech recognition or synthesis in isolation, with limited exploration of end-to-end spoken dialogue systems for Chinese dialects.\n117\t\n118\t3\n119\t\n120\tMethod\n121\t\n122\t3.1\n123\t\n124\tOverview\n125\t\n126\tWe propose an efficient framework for low-resource dialect adaptation in end-toend speech dialogue models. The framework consists of a dialect speech dialogue\n127\tsynthesis pipeline and a two-stage post-training procedure, including mixed-data\n128\tsupervised fine-tuning and self-aligned speech supervision training. Through the\n129\tproposed framework, pretrained speech dialogue models can acquire stable dialect understanding and generation capabilities while preserving their original\n130\tmultilingual ability.\n131\t3.2\n132\t\n133\tDialect Speech Dialogue Synthesis Pipeline\n134\t\n135\tThe proposed synthesis pipeline consists of three stages: dialect text generation,\n136\tdialect speech synthesis, and speech naturalness filtering.\n137\t\n138\t\f4\n139\t\n140\tY. Shu et al.\n141\t\n142\tDialect Dialogue Generation Directly prompting large language models to\n143\tgenerate dialect dialogues often produces repetitive and low-diversity content.\n144\tInstead, we rewrite the dialogue portion of an open-source Mandarin dialogue\n145\tdataset [17] into dialectal expressions using large language models [22] while\n146\tpreserving the original semantics and dialogue structure. This strategy efficiently\n147\treuses existing dialogue corpora and enables low-cost construction of diverse\n148\tdialect dialogue texts.\n149\tDialect Speech Synthesis We adopt different synthesis strategies for userquery speech and system-response speech. For user-query speech, we select 100\n150\tdialect seed utterances for each dialect from open-source dialect datasets [15,9,4],\n151\tincluding both male and female speakers. These utterances are used as reference\n152\taudio for CosyVoice2 [7] to synthesize dialect query speech from the rewritten\n153\tdialogue texts, ensuring diverse vocal characteristics in the input speech. For\n154\tsystem-response speech, we adopt a consistent speaker timbre strategy for response synthesis. Specifically, we first construct dialect-specific reference speech\n155\tconditioned on a target speaker and then use the generated reference speech\n156\tfor response synthesis. This strategy maintains consistent voice characteristics\n157\tin synthesized responses and further improves dialect expressiveness and speech\n158\tnaturalness. Further seed-set details are provided in Appendix A.\n159\tSpeech Naturalness Filtering To improve data quality, we further introduce\n160\ta speech filtering stage using UTMOS [13] to automatically remove synthesized\n161\tsamples with unclear pronunciation or unnatural prosody. Filtering details are\n162\tprovided in Appendix A.\n163\t3.3\n164\t\n165\tModel Training\n166\t\n167\tMost existing end-to-end speech-to-speech dialogue models [23,19,6,18,17,20,16]\n168\tare built upon pretrained text language models and synchronously generate both\n169\ttext and speech responses from speech inputs. In this work, we interpret such\n170\tsystems from a unified Thinker-Talker perspective. Specifically, given an input\n171\tspeech sequence X, the Thinker module performs speech understanding and\n172\tsemantic reasoning, producing intermediate hidden representations H together\n173\twith text response tokens T. Conditioned on H, the Talker module learns the\n174\tmapping from hidden semantic representations to speech response tokens U by\n175\tleveraging the semantic and paralinguistic information encoded in the hidden\n176\tstates. The overall architecture is illustrated in Figure 1.\n177\tAlthough these models effectively leverage pretrained language models for\n178\tend-to-end speech interaction, adapting them to low-resource dialect scenarios\n179\toften introduces shifts in the hidden-state distribution during post-training. Such\n180\tshifts are not necessarily undesirable, since the model must gradually acquire\n181\tnew dialect-specific semantic and acoustic representations. However, the speech\n182\tsupervision used during training often remains semantically inconsistent with\n183\tthe evolved hidden representations. As a result, the Talker module must implicitly perform semantic recovery in addition to speech generation, increasing\n184\toptimization difficulty and degrading speech quality.\n185\t\n186\t\fDialectS2S for Low-Resource Chinese Dialects\n187\t\n188\t5\n189\t\n190\tFig. 1. The proposed unified Thinker-Talker architecture.\n191\t\n192\tAs illustrated in Figure 2, we visualize the\n193\thidden-state distributions using PCA projection\n194\ton the token-averaged final-layer Thinker representations. The hidden representations continuously\n195\tevolve after supervised fine-tuning, highlighting the\n196\tnecessity of adapting speech supervision to the updated semantic space.\n197\tTo better align speech supervision with the evolv- Fig. 2. Visualization of the\n198\ting semantic representations during dialect adap- final-layer Thinker hidden\n199\ttation, we adopt a two-stage training framework states before and after suconsisting of Mixed-Data SFT and Self-aligned pervised fine-tuning.\n200\tSpeech Supervision Training. The first stage enables the model to acquire\n201\tdialect-aware semantic representations through supervised fine-tuning, while the\n202\tsecond stage further aligns speech generation with the evolved hidden representations to improve dialect speech quality and intelligibility.\n203\tMixed-Data SFT Initialized from a speech dialogue model [17] with strong\n204\tconversational performance in mainstream languages, we perform full-parameter\n205\tsupervised fine-tuning on mixed dialect, Mandarin, and English speech dialogue\n206\tdata. The training objective is defined as:\n207\t\n208\tLSF T = −\n209\t\n210\tL\n211\tX\n212\t\n213\tlog P (ti | X, T<i ; θT hinker ) −\n214\t\n215\ti=1\n216\t\n217\t|\n218\t\n219\tV\n220\tX\n221\t\n222\tlog P (uj | H, U<j ; θT alker ) (1)\n223\t\n224\tj=1\n225\t\n226\t{z\n227\t\n228\tThinker Loss\n229\t\n230\t}\n231\t\n232\t|\n233\t\n234\t{z\n235\t\n236\tTalker Loss\n237\t\n238\t}\n239\t\n240\twhere θT hinker and θT alker denote the parameters of the Thinker and Talker\n241\tmodules.\n242\tThis stage gradually shifts the hidden representations of the Thinker module\n243\ttoward dialect-aware semantic spaces through supervised fine-tuning, enabling\n244\tthe model to acquire initial dialect interaction capabilities. However, the Talker\n245\t\n246\t\f6\n247\t\n248\tY. Shu et al.\n249\t\n250\tmodule still struggles to fully adapt to the evolved semantic representations,\n251\tsince the representation space of the Thinker module continues to evolve during\n252\ttraining while the speech supervision remains unchanged.\n253\tSelf-aligned Speech Supervision Training To better align speech supervision with the evolved semantic representations of the Thinker module, we further\n254\tpropose self-aligned speech supervision training.\n255\tWe first use the fine-tuned Thinker module to generate text predictions:\n256\tT̂ = Thinker(X)\n257\t\n258\t(2)\n259\t\n260\tAligned speech supervision is then synthesized from the predicted text using\n261\ta dialect TTS model:\n262\tÛ = TTS(T̂)\n263\t\n264\t(3)\n265\t\n266\tThe synthesized speech is subsequently used as the new supervision target,\n267\tsuch that the semantic content of the speech supervision is explicitly aligned\n268\twith the semantic information encoded in the hidden representations for Talker\n269\tmodeling. To maintain stable coordination between the Thinker and Talker modules, the self-aligned stage still adopts end-to-end full-parameter optimization,\n270\talthough the self-aligned loss is primarily applied to the Talker module.\n271\tLAlign_T alker = −\n272\t\n273\tV\n274\tX\n275\t\n276\tlog P (ûj | H, Û<j ; θT alker )\n277\t\n278\t(4)\n279\t\n280\tj=1\n281\t\n282\tFig. 3. Self-aligned supervision aligns speech targets with semantic representations\n283\tproduced by the Thinker module, enabling the Talker module to focus on prosody and\n284\tpronunciation modeling.\n285\t\n286\tBy aligning speech supervision with the model’s semantic predictions, the\n287\tproposed strategy reduces semantic mismatch between hidden representations\n288\t\n289\t\fDialectS2S for Low-Resource Chinese Dialects\n290\t\n291\t7\n292\t\n293\tand speech targets, allowing the Talker module to focus on speech generation\n294\trather than semantic correction. Figure 3 provides a comparison between conventional supervised fine-tuning and the proposed self-aligned speech supervision\n295\tstrategy.\n296\t\n297\t4\n298\t\n299\tExperiments\n300\t\n301\t4.1\n302\t\n303\tDatasets\n304\t\n305\tBased on a high-quality open-source speech dialogue dataset [17], we construct\n306\tdialect speech dialogue corpora for three Chinese dialects, including Sichuanese,\n307\tCantonese, and the Tianjin dialect, using the proposed synthesis pipeline. Each\n308\tdialect contains approximately 3,600 speech query-response pairs. In addition,\n309\t3,600 high-MOS speech dialogue samples are selected for both Mandarin and\n310\tEnglish from this dataset and mixed with the constructed dialect data for joint\n311\ttraining. The final training set contains approximately 18,000 speech dialogue\n312\tpairs with a total duration of 225 hours, primarily consisting of open-domain\n313\tdaily conversations to better reflect real-world multilingual and dialect interaction scenarios.\n314\t4.2\n315\t\n316\tTraining Setup\n317\t\n318\tWe initialize the model from OpenS2S [17] due to its fully open-source nature\n319\tand strong conversational capabilities in everyday spoken interactions. We first\n320\tperform two epochs of supervised fine-tuning to obtain a base model with stable dialect capabilities, followed by self-aligned speech supervision training on\n321\tthe same dataset. All experiments are conducted on 8 NVIDIA A800 GPUs\n322\tusing DeepSpeed [12] with ZeRO Stage-2 [11]. The model is optimized using\n323\tAdamW [10] with a learning rate of 2 × 10−5 , β1 = 0.9, β2 = 0.999, ϵ = 1 × 10−8 ,\n324\tand weight decay 0.05. The learning rate schedule follows WarmupDecayLR.\n325\t\n326\t5\n327\t\n328\tEvaluation\n329\t\n330\t5.1\n331\t\n332\tEvaluation Setup\n333\t\n334\tWe evaluate DialectS2S on multilingual speech interaction from three aspects:\n335\tlanguage matching accuracy, response quality, and speech intelligibility. A multilingual dialect speech dialogue benchmark containing 250 test samples is constructed, covering two mainstream languages and three Chinese dialects, with\n336\tevaluation queries built using different reference audios and query texts. Comparisons are conducted against several representative open-source speech dialogue\n337\tmodels, including GLM-4-Voice [23], Qwen2.5-Omni [19], Kimi-Audio [6], StepAudio2 [18], OpenS2S [17], and MiniCPM-o-4.5 [3].\n338\t\n339\t\f8\n340\t\n341\t5.2\n342\t\n343\tY. Shu et al.\n344\t\n345\tResponse Language Matching Accuracy\n346\t\n347\tWe first evaluate whether the model can generate speech responses in the input\n348\tlanguage or dialect while preserving multilingual capability. Specifically, we use\n349\tFireRedLID [21] to identify the language of generated speech responses. All evaluations are conducted in a zero-shot setting without text prompts or explicit\n350\tlanguage control signals. Language matching accuracy is computed as the proportion of valid samples whose predicted language matches the input language.\n351\tResults are shown in Table 1. DialectS2S achieves substantial improvements\n352\tacross all dialect scenarios while maintaining strong Mandarin and English performance, significantly outperforming existing open-source baselines.\n353\tTable 1. Comparison of language matching accuracy (%) between DialectS2S and\n354\tbaseline models.\n355\tModel\n356\t\n357\tEnglish\n358\t\n359\tGLM-4-Voice\n360\tQwen2.5-Omni\n361\tKimi-Audio\n362\tStep-Audio2\n363\tOpenS2S\n364\tMiniCPM-o-4.5\n365\tDialectS2S\n366\t\n367\t94.00\n368\t100.00\n369\t72.00\n370\t100.00\n371\t100.00\n372\t100.00\n373\t100.00\n374\t\n375\t5.3\n376\t\n377\tLanguage Matching Accuracy\n378\tMandarin Sichuanese Cantonese Tianjin\n379\t97.92\n380\t100.00\n381\t100.00\n382\t86.00\n383\t93.88\n384\t98.00\n385\t97.96\n386\t\n387\t2.22\n388\t0.00\n389\t16.00\n390\t0.00\n391\t0.00\n392\t2.04\n393\t96.00\n394\t\n395\t46.94\n396\t0.00\n397\t0.00\n398\t10.00\n399\t0.00\n400\t52.21\n401\t95.00\n402\t\n403\t0.00\n404\t0.00\n405\t0.00\n406\t0.00\n407\t0.00\n408\t2.00\n409\t91.00\n410\t\n411\tAvg.\n412\t48.22\n413\t40.00\n414\t37.60\n415\t39.20\n416\t38.78\n417\t50.85\n418\t95.99\n419\t\n420\tResponse Quality\n421\t\n422\tResponse quality is further evaluated on the constructed multilingual dialect\n423\tbenchmark using the text responses synchronously generated during speech interaction. Qwen3-Plus [22] is employed as the automatic evaluator to assess naturalness, semantic accuracy, and conformity to dialectal language usage, with\n424\tscores ranging from 1 to 5. The scoring rubric is provided in Appendix A. Since\n425\tall compared models support simultaneous text and speech generation, no additional ASR system is required. As shown in Table 2, DialectS2S achieves the\n426\thighest overall average score and consistently delivers superior response quality across dialect scenarios, demonstrating strong semantic understanding and\n427\tdialect-aware response generation capability.\n428\t\n429\t\fDialectS2S for Low-Resource Chinese Dialects\n430\t\n431\t9\n432\t\n433\tTable 2. Response quality evaluation across different language and dialect scenarios.\n434\tModel\n435\tGLM-4-Voice\n436\tQwen2.5-Omni\n437\tKimi-Audio\n438\tStep-Audio2\n439\tOpenS2S\n440\tMiniCPM-o-4.5\n441\tDialectS2S\n442\t\n443\t5.4\n444\t\n445\tEnglish\n446\t4.56\n447\t4.60\n448\t4.07\n449\t4.20\n450\t4.54\n451\t4.22\n452\t4.60\n453\t\n454\tResponse Quality\n455\tMandarin Sichuanese Cantonese\n456\t4.40\n457\t4.65\n458\t4.12\n459\t3.92\n460\t4.36\n461\t4.45\n462\t4.33\n463\t\n464\t3.10\n465\t3.12\n466\t3.22\n467\t2.78\n468\t2.96\n469\t3.51\n470\t4.48\n471\t\n472\t4.17\n473\t3.26\n474\t3.80\n475\t3.02\n476\t3.26\n477\t4.24\n478\t4.28\n479\t\n480\tTianjin\n481\t\n482\tAvg.\n483\t\n484\t2.19\n485\t3.11\n486\t2.26\n487\t2.06\n488\t2.60\n489\t2.30\n490\t4.40\n491\t\n492\t3.68\n493\t3.75\n494\t3.49\n495\t3.20\n496\t3.54\n497\t3.74\n498\t4.42\n499\t\n500\tSpeech Intelligibility\n501\t\n502\tWe use character error rate (CER) to measure speech intelligibility, where lower\n503\tCER indicates more intelligible speech outputs. The text responses generated\n504\tby the model are used as references, while FRASR2-AED [21] transcribes the\n505\tgenerated speech responses for CER computation. Previous experiments show\n506\tthat existing baseline models mainly generate Mandarin Chinese rather than\n507\tdialect speech under dialect interaction settings. Therefore, CER evaluation is\n508\tinstead conducted against the average CER reported by the FRASR2-AED dialect benchmark.\n509\tAs shown in Table 3, DialectS2S achieves Table 3. CER (%) of generated\n510\tlow CER across both Mandarin and dialect speech compared with the average\n511\tscenarios, demonstrating strong speech intel- CER reported by the FRASR2-AED\n512\tligibility. In particular, DialectS2S achieves benchmark.\n513\tlower CER than the average performance reMandarin Dialect\n514\tported by the FRASR2-AED dialect bench3.28\n515\t8.69\n516\tmark in dialect settings, indicating highly in- DialectS2S\n517\tBenchmark Avg.\n518\t3.05\n519\t11.67\n520\ttelligible generated dialect speech.\n521\t\n522\t6\n523\t\n524\tAblation Study\n525\t\n526\tTo evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed self-aligned speech supervision strategy, we conduct ablation studies under three training configurations: sft-2ep,\n527\twhich applies two epochs of supervised fine-tuning; sft-3ep, which continues\n528\tsupervised fine-tuning for one additional epoch; and DialectS2S, which applies\n529\tself-aligned speech supervision on top of the sft-2ep model. Notably, sft-3ep and\n530\tDialectS2S are trained with comparable numbers of optimization steps, enabling\n531\ta fair comparison between continued conventional fine-tuning and the proposed\n532\ttraining strategy.\n533\tWe evaluate both response quality and speech intelligibility, with results reported in Table 4 and Table 5, respectively. As shown in Table 4, DialectS2S\n534\tachieves response quality comparable to sft-3ep, indicating that self-aligned\n535\t\n536\t\f10\n537\t\n538\tY. Shu et al.\n539\t\n540\tspeech supervision does not compromise semantic generation capability. In contrast, Table 5 shows that DialectS2S consistently outperforms both sft-2ep and\n541\tsft-3ep in speech intelligibility. In particular, simply extending supervised finetuning does not yield consistent improvements in CER and may even lead to\n542\tdegradation in certain dialect settings.\n543\tThese results suggest that increasing the amount of supervised fine-tuning\n544\talone is insufficient to address the hidden-state distribution shift in the ThinkerTalker architecture. In comparison, the proposed self-aligned speech supervision\n545\texplicitly aligns speech targets with the model’s semantic predictions, thereby\n546\treducing semantic mismatch and improving speech clarity and stability.\n547\tTable 4. Response quality evaluation under different training strategies across different\n548\tlanguage and dialect scenarios.\n549\tModel\n550\t\n551\tEnglish\n552\t\n553\tsft-2ep\n554\tsft-3ep\n555\tDialectS2S\n556\t\n557\t3.97\n558\t4.27\n559\t4.60\n560\t\n561\tResponse Quality\n562\tMandarin Sichuanese Cantonese\n563\t4.19\n564\t4.40\n565\t4.33\n566\t\n567\t4.02\n568\t4.67\n569\t4.48\n570\t\n571\t3.81\n572\t4.19\n573\t4.28\n574\t\n575\tTianjin\n576\t\n577\tAvg.\n578\t\n579\t4.36\n580\t4.57\n581\t4.40\n582\t\n583\t4.07\n584\t4.42\n585\t4.42\n586\t\n587\tTable 5. Comparison of CER (%) under different training strategies.\n588\tModel\n589\t\n590\tEnglish\n591\t\n592\tsft-2ep\n593\tsft-3ep\n594\tDialectS2S\n595\t\n596\t8.68\n597\t11.49\n598\t5.56\n599\t\n600\t7\n601\t\n602\tASR CER ↓\n603\tMandarin Sichuanese Cantonese\n604\t5.25\n605\t6.85\n606\t3.28\n607\t\n608\t11.29\n609\t9.85\n610\t8.63\n611\t\n612\t17.10\n613\t19.80\n614\t9.53\n615\t\n616\tTianjin\n617\t\n618\tAvg.\n619\t\n620\t8.43\n621\t8.36\n622\t7.92\n623\t\n624\t10.15\n625\t11.27\n626\t6.98\n627\t\n628\tConclusion\n629\t\n630\tThis work presents DialectS2S, an end-to-end speech dialogue model for lowresource Chinese dialects. By combining a scalable dialect data synthesis pipeline\n631\twith a two-stage post-training strategy, DialectS2S enables stable and intelligible dialect speech interaction under limited supervision. Experimental results\n632\tdemonstrate strong language and dialect matching, response quality, and text–\n633\tspeech consistency across three Chinese dialects. To facilitate future research and\n634\tpractical applications, we fully open-source the DialectS2S framework, including\n635\tmodel checkpoints, training datasets, and fine-tuning code. In future work, we\n636\tplan to support more dialects and explore more effective training strategies for\n637\timproving dialect authenticity and expressiveness.\n638\t\n639\t\fDialectS2S for Low-Resource Chinese Dialects\n640\t\n641\t11\n642\t\n643\tReferences\n644\t1. An, K., Chen, Y., Chen, Z., Deng, C., Du, Z., Gao, C., Gao, Z., Gong, B., Li, X.,\n645\tLi, Y., et al.: Fun-asr technical report. arXiv preprint arXiv:2509.12508 (2025)\n646\t2. Chen, Z., Chen, G., Wang, Y., Ding, C., Zhang, W.Q., et al.: Diamoe-tts: A unified\n647\tipa-based dialect tts framework with mixture-of-experts and parameter-efficient\n648\tzero-shot adaptation. arXiv preprint arXiv:2509.22727 (2025)\n649\t3. Cui, J., Xu, B., Wang, C., Yu, T., Sun, W., Xu, Y., Wang, T., He, Z., Ma, W., Cai,\n650\tT.,